1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,880
Okay, next section, the esophagus.

2
00:00:02,060 --> 00:00:04,160
The very first disorder that we want to talk about

3
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:07,020
is a TE fistula, a tracheoesophageal fistula.

4
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:08,860
Let's remind ourselves that a fistula

5
00:00:08,860 --> 00:00:11,160
is an abnormal connection between two tubes,

6
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:12,600
and in this particular case,

7
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:14,760
the abnormal connection is going to be

8
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:16,980
between the trachea and the esophagus,

9
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:19,100
and of course, this is a congenital defect.

10
00:00:19,660 --> 00:00:21,780
Now, there are multiple different variants,

11
00:00:21,940 --> 00:00:24,480
however, we're just going to focus on the most common variant

12
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:26,020
and go through a couple principles.

13
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:27,720
So in the most common variant,

14
00:00:27,720 --> 00:00:30,660
what we have is we have a proximal esophagus

15
00:00:30,660 --> 00:00:31,840
that is atretic.

16
00:00:32,540 --> 00:00:35,140
Atretic means that it ends in a blind pouch

17
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:36,060
or a blind loop,

18
00:00:36,140 --> 00:00:38,540
so we have proximal esophageal atresia,

19
00:00:38,860 --> 00:00:40,960
and then when you look at the distal esophagus,

20
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:42,780
it comes off the trachea,

21
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:44,580
so up here would be the trachea,

22
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:46,840
and then you've got the distal esophagus

23
00:00:46,840 --> 00:00:48,560
coming off the trachea,

24
00:00:48,620 --> 00:00:49,800
and that's where the fistula is.

25
00:00:49,860 --> 00:00:52,420
There's a connection between the distal esophagus

26
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:53,420
and the trachea.

27
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:55,200
Now, in this particular case,

28
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:58,200
the clinical findings are all based on this anatomy,

29
00:00:58,360 --> 00:00:59,560
so once you understand the anatomy,

30
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:00,500
everything is downhill.

31
00:01:01,020 --> 00:01:03,540
You can imagine that when the baby consumes food,

32
00:01:03,860 --> 00:01:05,580
it's going to go into this blind pouch,

33
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:07,040
and then it's going to shoot back up,

34
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:09,740
so the patient or the baby is going to have vomiting.

35
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:13,160
Another important feature of having this atresia

36
00:01:13,160 --> 00:01:14,520
of the esophagus is that,

37
00:01:14,860 --> 00:01:17,320
remember that the baby folds around in amniotic fluid,

38
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:19,760
and the amount of amniotic fluid

39
00:01:19,760 --> 00:01:21,960
is basically a function of two things.

40
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:24,920
The baby produces amniotic fluid from the kidneys.

41
00:01:25,080 --> 00:01:28,940
Remember that the vast majority of amniotic fluid

42
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:30,580
is actually the urine of the baby,

43
00:01:30,960 --> 00:01:31,760
and at the same time,

44
00:01:31,860 --> 00:01:34,040
the baby consumes some of that amniotic fluid

45
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:35,040
by swallowing it,

46
00:01:35,340 --> 00:01:36,920
and when the amniotic fluid is swallowed,

47
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:39,320
it then gets digested and goes through the GI tract,

48
00:01:39,320 --> 00:01:41,820
and so you can imagine that if the baby

49
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:43,440
can't swallow amniotic fluid,

50
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:45,040
they won't be able to remove

51
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:46,640
some of that fluid physiologically,

52
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:49,280
and hence there's going to be excess fluid,

53
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:51,360
and that's called polyhydramnios,

54
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:52,820
excess amniotic fluid,

55
00:01:53,140 --> 00:01:54,720
so that's the second clinical feature.

56
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:56,800
The third clinical feature will be that

57
00:01:56,800 --> 00:01:58,220
as the baby breathes,

58
00:01:58,280 --> 00:02:00,920
air will come down into the distal esophagus

59
00:02:00,920 --> 00:02:02,320
and eventually go into the stomach,

60
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:04,360
and so you'll get abdominal distension,

61
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:05,520
and then finally,

62
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:06,980
some of the contents from the stomach

63
00:02:06,980 --> 00:02:09,280
will aspirate back through the distal esophagus

64
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:10,620
into the trachea,

65
00:02:10,780 --> 00:02:12,180
which will then give you aspiration.

66
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:14,140
So there are four clinical features

67
00:02:14,140 --> 00:02:15,400
in this most common variant,

68
00:02:15,560 --> 00:02:18,060
and I've written this all very clearly in your text,

69
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:20,180
so you should be able to follow it there.

70
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:23,560
Here's an image of a TE fistula.

71
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:24,960
It's a beautiful gross picture.

72
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:27,380
You can clearly see that this is the proximal esophagus,

73
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:28,180
which is atretic.

74
00:02:28,380 --> 00:02:30,180
This is the distal esophagus here,

75
00:02:30,260 --> 00:02:32,420
and it's plugging into the trachea,

76
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:33,500
and so this is, again,

77
00:02:33,560 --> 00:02:35,740
the most common variant of a TE fistula.

78
00:02:35,740 --> 00:02:40,300
The esophageal web refers to a mucosal protrusion

79
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:44,200
that results in obstruction or a partial obstruction

80
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:46,580
with dysphagia for poorly chewed food.

81
00:02:46,940 --> 00:02:48,940
Let me just draw this out again just to remind you.

82
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:51,600
Let's say that that's the lumen of the esophagus there.

83
00:02:51,940 --> 00:02:53,980
Well, remember that the wall of the esophagus

84
00:02:53,980 --> 00:02:55,640
would contain a mucosa,

85
00:02:56,040 --> 00:02:58,700
and behind that mucosa would be a submucosa,

86
00:02:59,060 --> 00:03:00,360
and then behind the submucosa

87
00:03:00,360 --> 00:03:02,180
would be the muscularis propria,

88
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:04,580
and then behind the muscularis propria,

89
00:03:04,580 --> 00:03:05,560
you have a cirrhosa.

90
00:03:05,960 --> 00:03:09,260
So these are the four key layers of the GI tract,

91
00:03:09,640 --> 00:03:11,500
and in this particular case,

92
00:03:11,560 --> 00:03:12,620
in an esophageal web,

93
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:14,800
you get a protrusion of the mucosa,

94
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:17,780
which then results in a partial obstruction,

95
00:03:17,940 --> 00:03:18,720
which is called a web.

96
00:03:19,140 --> 00:03:20,900
Now, of course, when the patient eats,

97
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:25,040
especially if they have not chewed their food very well,

98
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:26,480
so if you have poorly chewed food,

99
00:03:26,780 --> 00:03:28,700
that can present with a little bit of dysphagia

100
00:03:28,700 --> 00:03:30,480
because it gets trapped in this web.

101
00:03:30,960 --> 00:03:32,080
So again, an esophageal web

102
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:34,800
is a thin protrusion of the mucosa only.

103
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:37,360
It occurs usually in the upper esophagus,

104
00:03:37,480 --> 00:03:39,880
and it presents with dysphagia for poorly chewed food.

105
00:03:40,260 --> 00:03:41,620
There is an increased risk,

106
00:03:41,660 --> 00:03:43,120
as I've stated here in your text,

107
00:03:43,240 --> 00:03:45,620
for squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus,

108
00:03:45,760 --> 00:03:47,340
but I'm gonna make this very clear

109
00:03:47,340 --> 00:03:48,700
at the end of the lecture.

110
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:50,500
So let's skip this point for now,

111
00:03:50,580 --> 00:03:52,280
but just bury it away in the back of your mind

112
00:03:52,280 --> 00:03:53,780
that in an esophageal web,

113
00:03:53,840 --> 00:03:56,120
you get an increased risk for squamous cell carcinoma.

114
00:03:57,200 --> 00:03:59,060
The most important manifestation,

115
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:01,300
at least for the purposes of board examinations,

116
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:02,700
of an esophageal web

117
00:04:02,700 --> 00:04:04,240
is something called Plummer-Vincent syndrome.

118
00:04:04,740 --> 00:04:07,520
Recall from our discussions in red blood cell pathology

119
00:04:07,520 --> 00:04:09,320
where we discussed this once before

120
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:11,120
that in Plummer-Vincent syndrome,

121
00:04:11,240 --> 00:04:14,160
the patients have severe iron deficiency anemia,

122
00:04:14,640 --> 00:04:17,340
an esophageal web, and a beefy red tongue.

123
00:04:17,640 --> 00:04:19,920
Now, that beefy red tongue is basically due to the fact

124
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:21,720
that they get atrophy of the mucosa,

125
00:04:21,720 --> 00:04:23,800
so they get a little bit of atrophic glossitis,

126
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:25,420
and the red color is actually

127
00:04:25,420 --> 00:04:27,260
because the blood vessels are exposed.

128
00:04:28,240 --> 00:04:30,620
The next disorder is called a Zenker diverticulum,

129
00:04:30,740 --> 00:04:33,280
and this is an outpouching of the pharyngeal mucosa

130
00:04:33,280 --> 00:04:35,260
through an acquired defect,

131
00:04:35,340 --> 00:04:37,320
acquired defect in the muscular wall.

132
00:04:37,620 --> 00:04:39,740
Basically, what happens is you have abnormal pressure

133
00:04:39,740 --> 00:04:41,980
in the pharynx when you're swallowing,

134
00:04:42,320 --> 00:04:44,760
usually due to some sort of impairment

135
00:04:44,760 --> 00:04:46,200
or disordered swallowing,

136
00:04:46,540 --> 00:04:48,060
and so when this abnormal pressure

137
00:04:48,060 --> 00:04:50,380
develops in the back of the pharynx,

138
00:04:50,380 --> 00:04:53,000
then you get a protrusion of the mucosa

139
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:54,080
through the muscular wall.

140
00:04:54,300 --> 00:04:55,700
So you can imagine it as such.

141
00:04:55,760 --> 00:04:57,320
If this is the mucosa here,

142
00:04:57,480 --> 00:04:58,520
this layer is the mucosa,

143
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:01,420
remember that behind it is going to be this muscular wall.

144
00:05:01,860 --> 00:05:03,380
Now, if there's disordered swallowing,

145
00:05:03,460 --> 00:05:05,640
you're going to generate some high pressure

146
00:05:05,640 --> 00:05:06,760
in this particular region,

147
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:09,020
and then that's going to result in a protrusion

148
00:05:09,020 --> 00:05:11,340
of the mucosa through or backward

149
00:05:11,340 --> 00:05:14,400
through the acquired defect in the muscular wall.

150
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:19,100
This usually occurs just above the upper esophageal sphincter

151
00:05:19,100 --> 00:05:22,100
at the junction of the esophagus and the pharynx,

152
00:05:22,220 --> 00:05:25,140
and this is the classic location.

153
00:05:25,580 --> 00:05:27,340
Now, patients can be asymptomatic.

154
00:05:28,180 --> 00:05:30,440
Perhaps the most common symptom that they can present with

155
00:05:30,440 --> 00:05:32,300
is that they feel like there's something

156
00:05:32,300 --> 00:05:33,460
in the back of their throat,

157
00:05:33,860 --> 00:05:35,780
but they can also have dysphagia.

158
00:05:36,280 --> 00:05:37,460
They can have obstruction,

159
00:05:37,840 --> 00:05:39,100
and they can have halitosis.

160
00:05:39,240 --> 00:05:40,980
Halitosis refers to bad breath,

161
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:44,660
and the idea here is that as the food gets trapped

162
00:05:44,660 --> 00:05:46,240
in that diverticulum, it can rot,

163
00:05:46,360 --> 00:05:47,080
and when it rots,

164
00:05:47,240 --> 00:05:49,000
then the smell of the rotting food

165
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:50,200
will actually come through the mouth,

166
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:52,280
giving the patient bad breath.

167
00:05:52,620 --> 00:05:54,280
Important to note that when we talk about

168
00:05:54,280 --> 00:05:55,420
this Zenker diverticulum,

169
00:05:55,540 --> 00:05:57,860
this is also known as a false diverticulum.

170
00:05:58,320 --> 00:06:01,380
False diverticulum means that you've only protruded

171
00:06:01,380 --> 00:06:04,520
one part of the wall through the entire wall.

172
00:06:05,040 --> 00:06:06,900
So that's also called a false diverticulum,

173
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:10,960
and most of the diverticulums in the GI tract

174
00:06:10,980 --> 00:06:12,600
are actually false diverticulums

175
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:14,420
where part of the wall goes through

176
00:06:14,420 --> 00:06:15,640
another part of the wall.

177
00:06:16,780 --> 00:06:18,320
Mallory-Weiss syndrome is when you get

178
00:06:18,320 --> 00:06:22,180
a longitudinal laceration of the esophageal mucosa

179
00:06:22,180 --> 00:06:23,120
at the GE junction,

180
00:06:23,580 --> 00:06:25,200
and the idea is as follows.

181
00:06:25,500 --> 00:06:26,980
If this is the esophagus,

182
00:06:27,060 --> 00:06:29,280
and you've got the stomach coming off the esophagus,

183
00:06:29,660 --> 00:06:32,360
of course, here is the GE junction.

184
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:34,740
Now, basically, in this particular disorder,

185
00:06:34,900 --> 00:06:36,280
patients have severe vomiting.

186
00:06:36,280 --> 00:06:37,920
As the vomit goes up,

187
00:06:37,920 --> 00:06:41,800
it also begins to create a linear laceration

188
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:44,520
of the mucosa at the GE junction.

189
00:06:44,920 --> 00:06:46,340
So that's called Mallory-Weiss syndrome.

190
00:06:46,480 --> 00:06:48,740
Now, of course, because this is all driven by vomiting,

191
00:06:49,080 --> 00:06:50,620
the causes are going to be those

192
00:06:50,620 --> 00:06:52,560
that are associated with severe vomiting.

193
00:06:53,060 --> 00:06:55,200
And so the two causes or the two circumstances

194
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:57,120
in which we usually see this

195
00:06:57,120 --> 00:06:59,620
are, number one, alcoholics or alcoholism

196
00:06:59,620 --> 00:07:01,320
because of the severe vomiting,

197
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:03,860
and then bulimics who force themselves to vomit

198
00:07:03,860 --> 00:07:07,000
in order to try to decrease or maintain their weight.

199
00:07:07,620 --> 00:07:10,240
And so that's what would then result

200
00:07:10,240 --> 00:07:13,580
in this linear longitudinal laceration

201
00:07:13,580 --> 00:07:16,240
of the mucosa at the GE junction.

202
00:07:16,680 --> 00:07:17,840
The way this is going to present

203
00:07:17,840 --> 00:07:19,820
is that this laceration is going to bleed,

204
00:07:19,900 --> 00:07:21,620
and so the patients are going to have hematemesis,

205
00:07:21,800 --> 00:07:22,820
which is blood and the vomit.

206
00:07:23,220 --> 00:07:24,880
However, it will also be painful,

207
00:07:24,880 --> 00:07:26,680
and that's particularly important

208
00:07:26,680 --> 00:07:28,300
is that these patients have,

209
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:29,560
as I've stated here in the text,

210
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:31,480
painful hematemesis.

211
00:07:32,060 --> 00:07:34,300
Now, there's a risk of something called Borhav syndrome,

212
00:07:34,480 --> 00:07:36,220
and Borhav syndrome is when you get rupture

213
00:07:36,220 --> 00:07:37,160
of the esophagus,

214
00:07:37,240 --> 00:07:38,980
so you can imagine that you can actually

215
00:07:38,980 --> 00:07:41,180
bore through the entire wall of the esophagus

216
00:07:41,180 --> 00:07:41,900
and get rupture.

217
00:07:42,460 --> 00:07:43,700
When you rupture the esophagus,

218
00:07:43,800 --> 00:07:44,980
you get air in the mediastinum.

219
00:07:45,380 --> 00:07:47,380
The air in the mediastinum can then actually

220
00:07:47,380 --> 00:07:49,320
track up through the fascial planes

221
00:07:49,320 --> 00:07:51,780
and eventually get into the subcutaneous tissue,

222
00:07:51,900 --> 00:07:53,420
especially in the region of the neck.

223
00:07:53,420 --> 00:07:55,200
Now, that will then result in something

224
00:07:55,200 --> 00:07:56,960
called subcutaneous emphysema,

225
00:07:57,080 --> 00:07:59,260
where you actually have air bubbles within the,

226
00:07:59,540 --> 00:08:01,220
you can actually push on the air bubbles

227
00:08:01,220 --> 00:08:03,140
within the, or beneath the skin,

228
00:08:03,560 --> 00:08:06,000
and as you push on those air bubbles beneath the skin,

229
00:08:06,240 --> 00:08:07,880
you can actually hear a crackling noise,

230
00:08:07,940 --> 00:08:09,300
almost like Rice Krispies,

231
00:08:09,660 --> 00:08:11,060
and so when you press on that,

232
00:08:11,120 --> 00:08:12,300
you get that crackling noise,

233
00:08:12,380 --> 00:08:17,280
and that is a classic sign of subcutaneous emphysema,

234
00:08:17,520 --> 00:08:18,780
which again, that would be a hint

235
00:08:18,780 --> 00:08:21,520
that you've got some sort of a process

236
00:08:21,520 --> 00:08:23,100
leading to air in the mediastinum,

237
00:08:23,100 --> 00:08:24,340
and in this particular case,

238
00:08:24,420 --> 00:08:25,860
we would think about Borhoff syndrome,

239
00:08:26,260 --> 00:08:29,080
so that's a very high-yield discussion to be aware of.

240
00:08:30,380 --> 00:08:31,940
Esophageal varices are when you get

241
00:08:31,940 --> 00:08:35,860
a dilated submucosal vein in the lower esophagus,

242
00:08:36,020 --> 00:08:37,740
and this is going to arise secondary

243
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:39,160
to portal hypertension.

244
00:08:39,620 --> 00:08:41,580
Let's remind ourselves of a little bit of anatomy.

245
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:43,320
Remember that there are two ways

246
00:08:43,320 --> 00:08:45,560
by which the veins of the esophagus drain.

247
00:08:45,900 --> 00:08:48,140
Most of the esophageal blood actually drains

248
00:08:48,140 --> 00:08:51,060
via the azagous vein into the superior vena cava,

249
00:08:51,620 --> 00:08:53,240
but however, there is some of the blood

250
00:08:53,240 --> 00:08:55,280
that drains from the left gastric vein

251
00:08:55,280 --> 00:08:56,360
into the portal vein,

252
00:08:56,440 --> 00:08:58,380
so that some of the blood from the esophagus

253
00:08:58,380 --> 00:09:00,000
does drain into the portal circuit.

254
00:09:00,660 --> 00:09:02,080
Now, if there's portal hypertension,

255
00:09:02,320 --> 00:09:03,980
then we're going to have a backup of blood

256
00:09:03,980 --> 00:09:05,700
into the left gastric vein,

257
00:09:05,800 --> 00:09:08,420
which will also result in a backup of blood

258
00:09:08,420 --> 00:09:10,980
into these veins in the esophagus

259
00:09:10,980 --> 00:09:13,220
that were draining via this pathway,

260
00:09:13,220 --> 00:09:16,120
and so that would result in a dilation of those veins,

261
00:09:16,440 --> 00:09:18,800
and so that's, again, called esophageal varices,

262
00:09:19,280 --> 00:09:22,600
dilated submucosal veins in the lower esophagus,

263
00:09:22,960 --> 00:09:24,720
of course, related to portal hypertension.

264
00:09:25,240 --> 00:09:26,420
Now, these are asymptomatic,

265
00:09:26,540 --> 00:09:27,700
so it's not too problematic.

266
00:09:27,940 --> 00:09:30,100
However, you've got to be very well aware

267
00:09:30,100 --> 00:09:32,420
of the complication, which is that these can rupture,

268
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:34,780
and if they rupture, the patient's going to present

269
00:09:34,780 --> 00:09:36,900
with hematemesis or the vomiting of blood.

270
00:09:37,200 --> 00:09:38,640
However, it will be painless.

271
00:09:38,780 --> 00:09:40,240
That's very important because, remember,

272
00:09:40,240 --> 00:09:42,180
when we talked about Mallory-Weiss syndrome,

273
00:09:42,560 --> 00:09:45,700
we said that the patients have painful hematemesis,

274
00:09:45,980 --> 00:09:47,300
whereas in esophageal varices,

275
00:09:47,460 --> 00:09:48,980
it's painless hematemesis.

276
00:09:49,460 --> 00:09:51,680
Now, this is very important to be aware of

277
00:09:51,680 --> 00:09:54,360
because it's the most common cause of death in cirrhosis,

278
00:09:54,720 --> 00:09:55,920
so patients who are cirrhotic

279
00:09:55,920 --> 00:09:57,460
obviously have portal hypertension,

280
00:09:57,960 --> 00:10:00,060
and the most common cause of death in those patients

281
00:10:00,060 --> 00:10:02,560
is rupture of an esophageal varice.

282
00:10:03,100 --> 00:10:05,740
And, of course, the second problem that these patients have

283
00:10:05,740 --> 00:10:07,880
is that they have a coagulopathy,

284
00:10:07,880 --> 00:10:10,660
so because their liver is not functioning

285
00:10:10,660 --> 00:10:12,760
and they're not producing their coag factors.

286
00:10:13,240 --> 00:10:15,200
And so this can also complicate further

287
00:10:15,200 --> 00:10:16,960
the fact that you now have got a bleed

288
00:10:16,960 --> 00:10:18,520
without the ability to seal it.

289
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:22,100
So most common cause of death in cirrhosis.

290
00:10:23,380 --> 00:10:23,820
Achalasia.

291
00:10:24,180 --> 00:10:26,440
A means without, and chalasia means relaxation.

292
00:10:26,840 --> 00:10:30,000
And so one of the key issues in this particular disorder

293
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,660
is that you lose the ability to relax

294
00:10:32,660 --> 00:10:34,260
the lower esophageal sphincter.

295
00:10:34,260 --> 00:10:37,380
In addition, there's disordered esophageal motility

296
00:10:37,380 --> 00:10:39,420
so that you lose motility as well.

297
00:10:39,920 --> 00:10:42,060
So these are the two key problems in achalasia.

298
00:10:42,220 --> 00:10:43,900
Number one, an inability to relax

299
00:10:43,900 --> 00:10:45,320
the lower esophageal sphincter.

300
00:10:45,680 --> 00:10:48,980
And number two, disordered esophageal motility.

301
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:51,700
So basically, the idea would be as follows,

302
00:10:51,860 --> 00:10:53,460
that if this is the esophagus,

303
00:10:54,340 --> 00:10:56,680
and of course the stomach is coming off the esophagus,

304
00:10:57,140 --> 00:10:58,280
you're well aware of the fact

305
00:10:58,280 --> 00:11:01,140
that in order to move food down this esophagus,

306
00:11:01,140 --> 00:11:04,640
both fluids and solids, fluid and solids

307
00:11:04,640 --> 00:11:07,900
both require peristalsis of this wall.

308
00:11:08,260 --> 00:11:10,440
So that if you lose peristalsis of this wall,

309
00:11:10,560 --> 00:11:12,140
then the patients are going to have dysphagia

310
00:11:12,140 --> 00:11:13,560
for both solids and liquids.

311
00:11:13,940 --> 00:11:15,580
And that's one of the key clinical features

312
00:11:15,580 --> 00:11:16,840
in this particular disorder,

313
00:11:17,120 --> 00:11:18,520
is that the patients have dysphagia

314
00:11:18,520 --> 00:11:19,480
for solids and liquids

315
00:11:19,480 --> 00:11:21,120
because they have an inability,

316
00:11:21,500 --> 00:11:24,360
or they have a loss of the esophageal motility.

317
00:11:24,680 --> 00:11:26,680
The other problem in this particular disorder

318
00:11:26,680 --> 00:11:28,380
is that they've got an inability

319
00:11:28,380 --> 00:11:30,900
to relax that lower esophageal sphincter.

320
00:11:31,260 --> 00:11:33,620
Now, if they can't relax that lower esophageal sphincter,

321
00:11:33,720 --> 00:11:34,640
then what's going to happen

322
00:11:34,640 --> 00:11:37,140
is that food is going to begin to pile up

323
00:11:37,140 --> 00:11:38,100
as it comes in,

324
00:11:38,160 --> 00:11:40,040
and you're going to get massive dilatation

325
00:11:40,040 --> 00:11:41,780
of the esophageal wall.

326
00:11:42,260 --> 00:11:43,820
You can do barium in these patients,

327
00:11:43,940 --> 00:11:46,000
and this massive dilatation will fill up

328
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:47,360
until you come to the point

329
00:11:47,360 --> 00:11:49,000
where there is this tightening

330
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:50,640
of the lower esophageal sphincter.

331
00:11:50,720 --> 00:11:51,520
I shouldn't say tightening.

332
00:11:51,640 --> 00:11:53,140
It's actually an inability to relax.

333
00:11:53,500 --> 00:11:55,460
So there is this inability to relax

334
00:11:55,460 --> 00:11:56,760
the lower esophageal sphincter.

335
00:11:58,380 --> 00:11:59,700
The lower esophageal hair looks like a bird beak,

336
00:12:00,060 --> 00:12:02,140
and so they call that the bird beak sign

337
00:12:02,140 --> 00:12:03,720
on barium swallow.

338
00:12:04,360 --> 00:12:05,940
Now, it's important to note

339
00:12:05,940 --> 00:12:07,300
what's causing this disorder.

340
00:12:07,600 --> 00:12:08,740
The cause of this disorder

341
00:12:08,740 --> 00:12:10,580
is the fact that the patients have damage

342
00:12:10,580 --> 00:12:13,040
to the ganglion cells in the myenteric plexus.

343
00:12:13,460 --> 00:12:14,940
Now, let's go back and remind ourselves

344
00:12:14,940 --> 00:12:16,880
of a little bit of normal histology,

345
00:12:16,960 --> 00:12:18,020
and remember that when we talk

346
00:12:18,020 --> 00:12:19,580
about the lumen of the esophagus,

347
00:12:20,020 --> 00:12:21,800
remember that the wall of the esophagus

348
00:12:21,800 --> 00:12:22,880
contains a mucosa,

349
00:12:23,360 --> 00:12:24,680
and then behind that mucosa,

350
00:12:24,760 --> 00:12:25,800
there's a submucosa,

351
00:12:25,800 --> 00:12:27,380
and then behind that submucosa,

352
00:12:27,520 --> 00:12:28,860
there's a muscularis propria,

353
00:12:29,200 --> 00:12:30,040
and then behind that,

354
00:12:30,140 --> 00:12:30,980
there is a serosa.

355
00:12:31,440 --> 00:12:32,720
Now, the muscularis propria

356
00:12:32,720 --> 00:12:34,240
is actually made up of two layers.

357
00:12:34,540 --> 00:12:36,060
There's an inner circular layer,

358
00:12:36,340 --> 00:12:38,080
and there's an outer longitudinal layer,

359
00:12:38,360 --> 00:12:40,120
and in between those two layers

360
00:12:40,120 --> 00:12:42,840
run something called the myenteric plexus.

361
00:12:43,220 --> 00:12:44,260
The myenteric plexus

362
00:12:44,260 --> 00:12:45,840
is a collection of ganglion cells,

363
00:12:46,160 --> 00:12:47,700
and that collection of ganglion cells

364
00:12:47,700 --> 00:12:49,080
is essential for regulating

365
00:12:49,080 --> 00:12:50,180
both bowel motility

366
00:12:50,180 --> 00:12:51,760
and for relaxing

367
00:12:51,760 --> 00:12:53,200
the lower esophageal sphincter.

368
00:12:53,200 --> 00:12:55,220
So if you destroy these ganglion cells,

369
00:12:55,440 --> 00:12:56,700
you're going to lose both

370
00:12:56,700 --> 00:12:57,820
bowel motility

371
00:12:57,820 --> 00:12:59,940
in the region of the esophagus

372
00:12:59,940 --> 00:13:01,000
because that's where it's occurring,

373
00:13:01,340 --> 00:13:02,920
and the inability to relax

374
00:13:02,920 --> 00:13:04,640
that lower esophageal sphincter.

375
00:13:05,580 --> 00:13:06,920
Now, we should then ask the question,

376
00:13:07,040 --> 00:13:07,820
what causes damage

377
00:13:07,820 --> 00:13:08,860
to these ganglion cells?

378
00:13:09,280 --> 00:13:10,940
Well, as I've stated here in your text,

379
00:13:11,240 --> 00:13:12,640
the damage to the ganglion cells

380
00:13:12,640 --> 00:13:13,780
can be idiopathic

381
00:13:13,780 --> 00:13:15,760
or secondary to a known insult,

382
00:13:15,840 --> 00:13:18,060
and one of the classic associations

383
00:13:18,060 --> 00:13:19,140
is Chagas disease

384
00:13:19,140 --> 00:13:20,660
where you can get damage

385
00:13:20,660 --> 00:13:21,720
of the ganglion cells

386
00:13:21,720 --> 00:13:25,060
via this infective process.

387
00:13:25,740 --> 00:13:26,940
Now, here's the classic picture

388
00:13:26,940 --> 00:13:28,100
of the barium swallow,

389
00:13:28,320 --> 00:13:29,440
and what I'm trying to highlight

390
00:13:29,440 --> 00:13:30,000
for you here

391
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:31,040
is the bird beak sign.

392
00:13:31,140 --> 00:13:32,140
It's a very pretty picture,

393
00:13:32,220 --> 00:13:32,980
the bird beak sign.

394
00:13:33,280 --> 00:13:34,840
This is the dilated wall

395
00:13:34,840 --> 00:13:35,820
of the esophagus,

396
00:13:35,960 --> 00:13:36,700
and this is where

397
00:13:36,700 --> 00:13:38,100
the lower esophageal sphincter

398
00:13:38,100 --> 00:13:39,000
is not relaxing.

399
00:13:39,440 --> 00:13:39,900
And so, again,

400
00:13:39,940 --> 00:13:40,680
this would produce

401
00:13:40,680 --> 00:13:42,440
what we classically call

402
00:13:42,440 --> 00:13:43,900
as a bird beak sign.

403
00:13:44,520 --> 00:13:45,480
Now, the clinical features

404
00:13:45,480 --> 00:13:46,260
of this disorder

405
00:13:46,260 --> 00:13:47,540
are all going to be based

406
00:13:47,540 --> 00:13:48,660
on what I've already explained.

407
00:13:48,660 --> 00:13:49,700
So, there'll be a dysphagia

408
00:13:49,700 --> 00:13:50,900
for both solids and liquids.

409
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:52,340
And again, you need motility

410
00:13:52,340 --> 00:13:53,980
to move both solids and liquids.

411
00:13:54,400 --> 00:13:55,500
They're going to have putrid breath,

412
00:13:55,540 --> 00:13:56,640
and that's because the solids

413
00:13:56,640 --> 00:13:57,480
are going to accumulate

414
00:13:57,480 --> 00:13:58,380
in the esophagus,

415
00:13:58,540 --> 00:13:59,080
and of course,

416
00:13:59,160 --> 00:14:00,520
that fluid is just going to rot,

417
00:14:00,620 --> 00:14:01,380
and the rotting fluid

418
00:14:01,380 --> 00:14:03,060
will then create very bad breath.

419
00:14:03,900 --> 00:14:04,820
An additional finding

420
00:14:04,820 --> 00:14:05,980
will be that there will be

421
00:14:05,980 --> 00:14:09,580
a high lower esophageal sphincter pressure.

422
00:14:09,920 --> 00:14:10,980
Now, the idea here, again,

423
00:14:11,040 --> 00:14:13,280
is that you've got this dilated wall,

424
00:14:13,380 --> 00:14:14,440
and then you've got tightness

425
00:14:14,440 --> 00:14:16,000
of the lower esophageal sphincter,

426
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:17,680
so that results in a high pressure

427
00:14:17,680 --> 00:14:20,020
when you do esophageal pressure studies.

428
00:14:20,380 --> 00:14:22,560
And this is a very high-yield association,

429
00:14:23,060 --> 00:14:24,860
and it comes up often on examination,

430
00:14:24,860 --> 00:14:25,920
so you need to know

431
00:14:25,920 --> 00:14:28,140
that the pressure and the LES will be high.

432
00:14:28,200 --> 00:14:29,520
And of course, I think that makes sense

433
00:14:29,520 --> 00:14:31,060
if you understand the disease.

434
00:14:31,500 --> 00:14:32,240
The bird beak sign

435
00:14:32,240 --> 00:14:33,180
I've already talked about,

436
00:14:33,440 --> 00:14:34,600
it's also important to note

437
00:14:34,600 --> 00:14:36,140
that there's an increased risk

438
00:14:36,140 --> 00:14:38,000
for esophageal squamous cell carcinoma,

439
00:14:38,160 --> 00:14:39,660
but again, I'm going to touch on that

440
00:14:39,660 --> 00:14:40,720
when we talk about cancer,

441
00:14:40,780 --> 00:14:41,680
and I'll make that principle

442
00:14:41,680 --> 00:14:42,780
very easy for you.

443
00:14:42,780 --> 00:14:45,540
The next disorder is called GERD,

444
00:14:45,800 --> 00:14:49,260
and GERD refers to gastroesophageal reflux disease.

445
00:14:49,640 --> 00:14:51,660
Now, the idea here is as follows,

446
00:14:52,020 --> 00:14:53,080
that what's going to happen

447
00:14:53,080 --> 00:14:54,660
is that you've got the esophagus,

448
00:14:54,740 --> 00:14:55,860
and then you've got the stomach,

449
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:57,520
and of course, the stomach is coming off.

450
00:14:57,600 --> 00:14:59,660
This is the lower esophageal sphincter,

451
00:15:00,140 --> 00:15:01,560
and the lower esophageal sphincter

452
00:15:01,560 --> 00:15:02,860
in this particular disorder

453
00:15:02,860 --> 00:15:05,500
is not as tight as it should be,

454
00:15:05,760 --> 00:15:08,640
so there's a decrease in the tone

455
00:15:08,640 --> 00:15:10,320
of the lower esophageal sphincter.

456
00:15:10,320 --> 00:15:13,480
What then happens is that allows for acid

457
00:15:13,480 --> 00:15:15,400
and gastric contents to shoot up

458
00:15:15,400 --> 00:15:16,600
into the esophagus.

459
00:15:16,780 --> 00:15:18,620
When that acid and gastric contents

460
00:15:18,620 --> 00:15:20,660
shoot up into the esophagus,

461
00:15:20,980 --> 00:15:22,680
that then is going to change the stress

462
00:15:22,680 --> 00:15:24,720
on the cells of the lower esophagus

463
00:15:24,720 --> 00:15:27,360
because now they're seeing acid instead of food,

464
00:15:27,680 --> 00:15:28,840
and when they see that acid,

465
00:15:28,940 --> 00:15:30,380
they then undergo a metaplasia

466
00:15:30,380 --> 00:15:32,140
to better deal with the stress.

467
00:15:32,660 --> 00:15:34,680
Now, so initially, remember that the cells

468
00:15:34,680 --> 00:15:35,900
that are lining the esophagus

469
00:15:35,900 --> 00:15:39,100
are non-keratinizing squamous epithelium.

470
00:15:39,100 --> 00:15:41,920
That's the normal lining of the esophagus,

471
00:15:42,100 --> 00:15:44,060
non-keratinizing squamous epithelium,

472
00:15:44,180 --> 00:15:45,300
but what's going to happen

473
00:15:45,300 --> 00:15:46,920
in response to this change in stress

474
00:15:46,920 --> 00:15:48,200
is that you're going to get a change

475
00:15:48,200 --> 00:15:49,020
in the cell type.

476
00:15:49,680 --> 00:15:50,720
The change in the cell type

477
00:15:50,720 --> 00:15:53,640
then becomes non-ciliated columnar cells

478
00:15:53,640 --> 00:15:55,160
with goblet cells,

479
00:15:55,520 --> 00:15:57,960
and that is then, of course, a metaplasia.

480
00:15:58,440 --> 00:15:59,760
Now, let's go back and just review

481
00:15:59,760 --> 00:16:01,680
what we have here in your text.

482
00:16:02,080 --> 00:16:04,080
So, of course, we've already talked about the fact

483
00:16:04,080 --> 00:16:05,920
that you get reflux of acid from the stomach

484
00:16:05,920 --> 00:16:08,080
due to reduced lower esophageal tone.

485
00:16:08,480 --> 00:16:09,760
The risk factors are interesting.

486
00:16:09,960 --> 00:16:13,920
Alcohol, tobacco, obesity, a fat-rich diet, caffeine.

487
00:16:14,300 --> 00:16:15,540
All of these are risk factors.

488
00:16:15,860 --> 00:16:17,620
One that we should particularly emphasize

489
00:16:17,620 --> 00:16:19,420
is something called a hiatal hernia.

490
00:16:19,760 --> 00:16:21,680
So let's take a minute to just explain

491
00:16:21,680 --> 00:16:23,200
what the hiatal hernia is.

492
00:16:23,560 --> 00:16:24,980
Now, basically, again,

493
00:16:25,120 --> 00:16:26,640
you should remember your normal anatomy,

494
00:16:26,800 --> 00:16:28,180
and that is that the esophagus

495
00:16:28,180 --> 00:16:30,060
is the plugging into the stomach, correct?

496
00:16:30,060 --> 00:16:32,260
And, of course, we know that running here

497
00:16:32,260 --> 00:16:34,400
and running here is the diaphragm,

498
00:16:34,600 --> 00:16:37,100
and the diaphragm plays a particularly important role

499
00:16:37,100 --> 00:16:38,280
in helping to maintain

500
00:16:38,280 --> 00:16:40,460
that lower esophageal sphincter, by the way.

501
00:16:40,940 --> 00:16:42,020
Now, what can happen is that

502
00:16:42,020 --> 00:16:43,820
you can get a diaphragmatic hernia

503
00:16:43,820 --> 00:16:46,060
in which the stomach can actually herniate

504
00:16:46,060 --> 00:16:47,220
up into the thorax.

505
00:16:47,580 --> 00:16:50,000
The most common type of diaphragmatic hernia

506
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,520
is actually called a sliding hiatal hernia,

507
00:16:52,940 --> 00:16:54,880
and what happens in this particular case

508
00:16:54,880 --> 00:16:56,620
is that the stomach here

509
00:16:56,620 --> 00:16:59,800
herniates upward into the esophagus,

510
00:17:00,340 --> 00:17:02,140
and that's the most common type again.

511
00:17:02,260 --> 00:17:03,980
It's called a sliding hiatal hernia.

512
00:17:04,460 --> 00:17:06,300
Now, this results in a couple things.

513
00:17:06,420 --> 00:17:07,880
Number one, you've now got

514
00:17:07,880 --> 00:17:09,320
a little bit of part of the stomach,

515
00:17:09,460 --> 00:17:11,640
which really here would be the cardia of the stomach.

516
00:17:11,900 --> 00:17:12,880
So the cardia of the stomach

517
00:17:12,880 --> 00:17:14,240
is up into the esophagus,

518
00:17:14,320 --> 00:17:15,880
and that's going to allow for reflux,

519
00:17:16,560 --> 00:17:18,520
because, again, you really don't have

520
00:17:18,520 --> 00:17:20,380
any sphincter now.

521
00:17:20,480 --> 00:17:21,620
You've just kind of got the stomach

522
00:17:21,620 --> 00:17:23,820
pressing up against the esophagus here,

523
00:17:23,880 --> 00:17:25,400
and so that's going to allow for reflux,

524
00:17:25,400 --> 00:17:27,300
which would then increase the risk

525
00:17:27,300 --> 00:17:29,160
for gastroesophageal reflux disease,

526
00:17:29,380 --> 00:17:30,860
and that's the context in which

527
00:17:30,860 --> 00:17:31,960
we're having this discussion.

528
00:17:32,560 --> 00:17:33,600
Another important thing,

529
00:17:33,680 --> 00:17:35,560
and that's high yield for examinations as well,

530
00:17:35,620 --> 00:17:36,920
is that you're still going to have

531
00:17:36,920 --> 00:17:38,660
the little bit of tone here

532
00:17:38,660 --> 00:17:40,120
in the lower esophageal sphincter.

533
00:17:40,200 --> 00:17:41,320
The lower esophageal sphincter

534
00:17:41,320 --> 00:17:43,120
is normal when you have this hernia,

535
00:17:43,500 --> 00:17:44,860
and so this produces something called

536
00:17:44,860 --> 00:17:46,920
the hourglass appearance of the stomach

537
00:17:46,920 --> 00:17:50,180
with sort of a compression down the middle here,

538
00:17:50,580 --> 00:17:52,040
and so that's an important thing

539
00:17:52,040 --> 00:17:53,540
to be familiar with as well.

540
00:17:53,540 --> 00:17:55,700
Remember that there is another possibility

541
00:17:55,700 --> 00:17:58,160
when you talk about diaphragmatic hernias,

542
00:17:58,640 --> 00:18:01,560
and that is that you could actually develop this,

543
00:18:01,640 --> 00:18:02,940
where you've got the esophagus

544
00:18:02,940 --> 00:18:04,380
and then the stomach coming off,

545
00:18:04,460 --> 00:18:05,920
and then you've got the diaphragm here.

546
00:18:06,200 --> 00:18:07,940
You could actually develop a hernia

547
00:18:07,940 --> 00:18:11,460
such that the stomach actually herniates

548
00:18:11,460 --> 00:18:13,180
up next to the esophagus.

549
00:18:13,580 --> 00:18:16,100
That's called a paraesophageal hernia.

550
00:18:17,060 --> 00:18:18,480
All right, paraesophageal,

551
00:18:18,560 --> 00:18:20,460
because you're herniating a portion of the stomach

552
00:18:20,460 --> 00:18:22,720
up next to the esophagus,

553
00:18:23,020 --> 00:18:25,740
and what's important about this paraesophageal hernia

554
00:18:25,740 --> 00:18:28,360
is that it is, number one, much less common

555
00:18:28,360 --> 00:18:29,940
than the sliding hiatal hernia

556
00:18:29,940 --> 00:18:31,020
that I just described,

557
00:18:31,400 --> 00:18:33,800
and number two, the classic association here

558
00:18:33,800 --> 00:18:37,360
is that you have bowel sounds in the lung fields,

559
00:18:37,420 --> 00:18:38,660
so in the lower lung field,

560
00:18:38,780 --> 00:18:40,260
which of course here would be the lung,

561
00:18:40,680 --> 00:18:42,180
you would actually have bowel sounds

562
00:18:42,180 --> 00:18:43,260
in the lower lung field,

563
00:18:43,560 --> 00:18:44,880
and another important consequence

564
00:18:44,880 --> 00:18:46,080
of having this type of hernia

565
00:18:46,080 --> 00:18:50,100
is that this hernia as it's present congenitally

566
00:18:50,100 --> 00:18:53,540
will result in less space for the lung to develop,

567
00:18:53,900 --> 00:18:56,180
and so these patients can also have lung hypoplasia.

568
00:18:56,480 --> 00:18:58,260
Now I'm making a little bit of a diversion here.

569
00:18:58,320 --> 00:19:00,440
In this particular paraesophageal hernia,

570
00:19:00,900 --> 00:19:03,020
you wouldn't necessarily have GERD,

571
00:19:03,260 --> 00:19:04,660
but I just want to talk about this

572
00:19:04,660 --> 00:19:05,520
for completeness sake

573
00:19:05,520 --> 00:19:07,420
because it does show up on examinations.

574
00:19:07,960 --> 00:19:09,200
Anyway, let's go back to GERD

575
00:19:09,200 --> 00:19:10,140
and just quickly review.

576
00:19:10,500 --> 00:19:11,500
So what are we saying here?

577
00:19:11,560 --> 00:19:13,100
We say that there's reflux of acid

578
00:19:13,100 --> 00:19:15,760
from the stomach due to reduced lower esophageal tone.

579
00:19:16,060 --> 00:19:17,360
I've told you about the risk factors,

580
00:19:17,460 --> 00:19:19,060
and we talked about the hiatal hernia

581
00:19:19,060 --> 00:19:20,900
in which the cardia of the stomach

582
00:19:20,900 --> 00:19:23,440
sort of balloons up into the esophagus,

583
00:19:23,500 --> 00:19:24,520
as I've already explained.

584
00:19:25,800 --> 00:19:27,740
Now the clinical features are all based on the fact

585
00:19:27,740 --> 00:19:29,540
that you're shooting acid into the esophagus.

586
00:19:29,640 --> 00:19:30,800
So the patients get heartburn,

587
00:19:30,880 --> 00:19:32,420
which mimics cardiac chest pain,

588
00:19:32,780 --> 00:19:34,360
and so anytime when a patient comes in

589
00:19:34,360 --> 00:19:36,060
with quote-unquote cardiac chest pain,

590
00:19:36,120 --> 00:19:38,040
you do want to think about heartburn

591
00:19:38,040 --> 00:19:39,320
in the back of your mind

592
00:19:39,320 --> 00:19:41,080
as a possibility for that chest pain.

593
00:19:41,080 --> 00:19:43,540
They also present with asthma and cough.

594
00:19:43,860 --> 00:19:45,380
Now what's interesting about this asthma

595
00:19:45,380 --> 00:19:47,700
is that it is adult-onset asthma.

596
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,480
This isn't asthma that the patient has as a child,

597
00:19:50,880 --> 00:19:52,100
but instead what happens is

598
00:19:52,100 --> 00:19:54,860
they get new-onset asthma as an adult.

599
00:19:55,680 --> 00:19:57,520
And so when you have adult-onset asthma,

600
00:19:57,760 --> 00:19:58,820
you really want to think about

601
00:19:58,820 --> 00:20:00,400
gastroesophageal reflux disease,

602
00:20:00,540 --> 00:20:01,480
and the idea here,

603
00:20:01,620 --> 00:20:03,900
if this is the stomach and the esophagus coming up,

604
00:20:04,140 --> 00:20:06,900
as you get shooting of acid up into the esophagus,

605
00:20:06,980 --> 00:20:08,760
you can irritate the airway as well,

606
00:20:08,760 --> 00:20:11,040
and that can result in asthma and cough.

607
00:20:11,420 --> 00:20:13,480
The acid can also reach the level of the teeth,

608
00:20:13,600 --> 00:20:15,220
which would then cause damage

609
00:20:15,220 --> 00:20:16,500
to the enamel of the teeth.

610
00:20:16,860 --> 00:20:18,000
So that's a clinical feature.

611
00:20:18,420 --> 00:20:19,540
Now some of the consequences

612
00:20:19,540 --> 00:20:21,420
of having all this acid shooting up

613
00:20:21,420 --> 00:20:23,460
into the lower esophagus

614
00:20:23,460 --> 00:20:24,400
is that number one,

615
00:20:24,460 --> 00:20:26,240
you can get ulceration with stricture.

616
00:20:26,640 --> 00:20:27,960
Again, the idea being

617
00:20:27,960 --> 00:20:30,300
that if you've got the esophagus here

618
00:20:30,300 --> 00:20:31,660
and then the stomach coming off

619
00:20:31,660 --> 00:20:32,880
and you're shooting up acid,

620
00:20:33,120 --> 00:20:35,500
you can damage the mucosa so that it ulcerates,

621
00:20:35,500 --> 00:20:37,460
and then when you ulcerate the mucosa,

622
00:20:37,760 --> 00:20:38,340
you've done,

623
00:20:38,440 --> 00:20:39,560
remember that an ulceration

624
00:20:39,560 --> 00:20:41,300
is a complete knockout of the mucosa,

625
00:20:41,640 --> 00:20:43,880
so that if you've got mucosa and submucosa,

626
00:20:44,220 --> 00:20:45,320
when you get an ulceration,

627
00:20:45,400 --> 00:20:47,400
you actually are taking a divot out of the mucosa

628
00:20:47,400 --> 00:20:49,340
along with a little piece of submucosa.

629
00:20:49,780 --> 00:20:50,980
If you knock out the mucosa,

630
00:20:51,100 --> 00:20:52,380
you're knocking out the stem cells,

631
00:20:52,440 --> 00:20:53,500
and the way by which ulcers

632
00:20:53,500 --> 00:20:55,480
are going to heal then is fibrosis.

633
00:20:55,740 --> 00:20:57,320
So you can actually get fibrosis

634
00:20:57,320 --> 00:20:58,760
of this lower region here,

635
00:20:59,120 --> 00:21:00,660
and that can result in a stricture,

636
00:21:00,660 --> 00:21:01,740
and a stricture, again,

637
00:21:01,820 --> 00:21:05,380
is going to result in a loss of the lumen

638
00:21:05,380 --> 00:21:08,160
or at least a narrowing of the lumen

639
00:21:08,160 --> 00:21:10,000
of the lower esophagus.

640
00:21:10,280 --> 00:21:11,440
So that's one complication,

641
00:21:11,660 --> 00:21:14,040
and that is that you can get ulceration with stricture.

642
00:21:14,560 --> 00:21:15,540
A second complication

643
00:21:15,540 --> 00:21:17,940
and a very high-yield complication, again,

644
00:21:18,020 --> 00:21:20,900
is that when you have all of this acid shooting up,

645
00:21:20,960 --> 00:21:22,160
you're going to get the metaplasia

646
00:21:22,160 --> 00:21:23,260
that I've already described,

647
00:21:23,580 --> 00:21:25,260
and that's called Barrett's esophagus.

648
00:21:25,800 --> 00:21:27,960
This is a very high-yield point to be aware of.

649
00:21:28,180 --> 00:21:29,720
Here's a picture of Barrett's esophagus.

650
00:21:29,720 --> 00:21:31,920
I place it here because it's a very common picture

651
00:21:31,920 --> 00:21:32,900
to show up on exams.

652
00:21:33,400 --> 00:21:36,880
Here is the non-keratinizing squamous epithelium

653
00:21:36,880 --> 00:21:38,880
that normally lines the esophagus,

654
00:21:39,040 --> 00:21:41,040
and we've got acid shooting up

655
00:21:41,040 --> 00:21:43,640
and then hitting this particular portion

656
00:21:43,640 --> 00:21:44,680
of the lower esophagus.

657
00:21:44,820 --> 00:21:48,060
This entire slide should represent a normal esophagus,

658
00:21:48,140 --> 00:21:48,740
and you can see

659
00:21:48,740 --> 00:21:51,180
that the non-keratinizing squamous epithelium

660
00:21:51,180 --> 00:21:52,320
has ended abruptly,

661
00:21:52,540 --> 00:21:53,420
and all of a sudden,

662
00:21:53,540 --> 00:21:55,080
we now have these columnar cells.

663
00:21:55,460 --> 00:21:56,320
And let me remind you

664
00:21:56,320 --> 00:21:58,220
that when you think about the metaplasia here,

665
00:21:58,220 --> 00:22:03,600
you get a metaplasia to a non-ciliated columnar epithelium

666
00:22:03,600 --> 00:22:04,640
with goblet cells.

667
00:22:04,780 --> 00:22:07,180
You've got to know that entire phrase,

668
00:22:07,660 --> 00:22:12,080
non-ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells.

669
00:22:12,180 --> 00:22:13,020
That's very important.

670
00:22:13,400 --> 00:22:16,260
And so here is this non-ciliated columnar epithelium

671
00:22:16,260 --> 00:22:18,420
with these interspersed goblet cells.

672
00:22:18,820 --> 00:22:19,340
Now, of course,

673
00:22:19,420 --> 00:22:21,100
this is a response of the stem cells

674
00:22:21,100 --> 00:22:22,260
to the acidic stress,

675
00:22:22,360 --> 00:22:24,140
and we've talked about this in detail

676
00:22:24,140 --> 00:22:25,500
in our metaplasia discussions.

677
00:22:25,500 --> 00:22:27,280
Because it's a metaplasia,

678
00:22:27,420 --> 00:22:28,960
it can progress to dysplasia,

679
00:22:29,340 --> 00:22:32,040
and that dysplasia could progress to adenocarcinoma.

680
00:22:32,560 --> 00:22:34,200
So that takes us into a discussion

681
00:22:34,200 --> 00:22:35,860
of esophageal carcinoma.

682
00:22:36,260 --> 00:22:37,440
But let's remind ourselves

683
00:22:37,440 --> 00:22:38,680
that esophageal carcinoma

684
00:22:38,680 --> 00:22:40,240
can be divided into two types,

685
00:22:40,360 --> 00:22:42,560
adenocarcinoma or squamous cell carcinoma.

686
00:22:43,060 --> 00:22:45,020
So in the case of adenocarcinoma,

687
00:22:45,060 --> 00:22:46,280
the most important things to know

688
00:22:46,280 --> 00:22:47,240
are that it's going to be

689
00:22:47,240 --> 00:22:48,860
a malignant proliferation of glands.

690
00:22:49,260 --> 00:22:51,340
Now, you don't normally have glands

691
00:22:51,340 --> 00:22:52,500
within the esophagus,

692
00:22:52,500 --> 00:22:54,360
and so the way by which it's going to occur

693
00:22:54,360 --> 00:22:55,840
is that you're going to get metaplasia,

694
00:22:56,060 --> 00:22:57,340
i.e. Barrett's esophagus,

695
00:22:57,760 --> 00:23:00,400
and which will then turn into an adenocarcinoma

696
00:23:00,400 --> 00:23:02,460
via the dysplasia carcinoma sequence.

697
00:23:02,960 --> 00:23:03,500
Now, of course,

698
00:23:03,620 --> 00:23:05,160
the only way to get this, of course,

699
00:23:05,220 --> 00:23:06,280
is going to be the metaplasia.

700
00:23:06,380 --> 00:23:07,500
Therefore, it's going to occur

701
00:23:07,500 --> 00:23:08,500
in the lower one-third,

702
00:23:08,620 --> 00:23:09,420
because that's exactly

703
00:23:09,420 --> 00:23:10,620
where the metaplasia occurs.

704
00:23:11,180 --> 00:23:12,280
Now, what I can tell you about adenocarcinoma

705
00:23:12,840 --> 00:23:14,800
is that it is the most common type

706
00:23:14,800 --> 00:23:16,960
of esophageal carcinoma in the West.

707
00:23:17,280 --> 00:23:19,280
So that's the summary of adenocarcinoma.

708
00:23:19,280 --> 00:23:21,940
Most common type of esophageal carcinoma in the West

709
00:23:21,940 --> 00:23:24,060
arises from Barrett's esophagus,

710
00:23:24,460 --> 00:23:26,300
lower one-third of the esophagus.

711
00:23:27,120 --> 00:23:29,440
So once you understand the basics about adenocarcinoma,

712
00:23:29,540 --> 00:23:31,140
let's quickly review what I say in the book.

713
00:23:31,220 --> 00:23:33,820
I say adenocarcinoma is a malignant proliferation of glands,

714
00:23:33,920 --> 00:23:36,260
most common type of esophageal carcinoma in the West,

715
00:23:36,680 --> 00:23:38,600
Barrett's esophagus, lower one-third.

716
00:23:39,220 --> 00:23:42,060
Now, the second type of esophageal carcinoma

717
00:23:42,060 --> 00:23:43,220
is squamous cell carcinoma.

718
00:23:43,420 --> 00:23:45,680
This is a malignant proliferation of squamous cells,

719
00:23:45,680 --> 00:23:49,580
and it is the most common esophageal cancer worldwide.

720
00:23:50,160 --> 00:23:51,440
It usually arises in the middle

721
00:23:51,440 --> 00:23:53,980
or upper third of the esophagus.

722
00:23:54,380 --> 00:23:55,760
Now, the risk factors are interesting,

723
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,220
and there's a long list of risk factors

724
00:23:58,220 --> 00:23:59,140
that we can go through,

725
00:23:59,340 --> 00:24:00,940
but let me just summarize this for you

726
00:24:00,940 --> 00:24:03,140
in just one word, irritation.

727
00:24:03,900 --> 00:24:05,980
If you irritate the squamous mucosa,

728
00:24:06,280 --> 00:24:07,740
you are going to increase the risk

729
00:24:07,740 --> 00:24:09,560
for development of squamous cell carcinoma.

730
00:24:09,800 --> 00:24:11,000
So let's talk about some of these

731
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:12,560
and see how they irritate the mucosa.

732
00:24:12,560 --> 00:24:15,180
Of course, alcohol and tobacco are irritants.

733
00:24:16,020 --> 00:24:17,420
We are well aware of the fact

734
00:24:17,420 --> 00:24:18,860
that they irritate epithelium,

735
00:24:18,980 --> 00:24:20,800
and so they will irritate the mucosa

736
00:24:20,800 --> 00:24:22,180
and result in an increased risk

737
00:24:22,180 --> 00:24:23,420
for squamous cell carcinoma.

738
00:24:23,820 --> 00:24:24,600
Very hot tea.

739
00:24:24,900 --> 00:24:27,180
Places in southern China and Iran

740
00:24:27,180 --> 00:24:29,260
where they tend to drink very hot tea,

741
00:24:29,580 --> 00:24:32,420
there's an increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma

742
00:24:32,420 --> 00:24:33,760
in those particular regions.

743
00:24:33,860 --> 00:24:35,520
You find a relatively high incidence

744
00:24:35,520 --> 00:24:36,760
of squamous cell carcinoma

745
00:24:36,760 --> 00:24:38,120
in that part of the world.

746
00:24:38,720 --> 00:24:40,660
Achalasia can also increase the risk.

747
00:24:40,660 --> 00:24:43,620
Now, how is achalasia going to result in irritation?

748
00:24:44,040 --> 00:24:45,260
Well, you're well aware of the fact

749
00:24:45,260 --> 00:24:47,980
that the problem in achalasia is two things.

750
00:24:48,140 --> 00:24:51,040
You have disordered esophageal motility

751
00:24:51,040 --> 00:24:52,920
and an inability to relax

752
00:24:52,920 --> 00:24:54,220
the lower esophageal sphincter

753
00:24:54,220 --> 00:24:56,020
so that the patients get this classic

754
00:24:56,020 --> 00:24:58,160
bird beak appearance of their esophagus.

755
00:24:58,500 --> 00:24:59,800
Now, imagine that basically

756
00:24:59,800 --> 00:25:01,580
the esophagus is full of food, correct?

757
00:25:01,920 --> 00:25:03,000
And remember that the food

758
00:25:03,000 --> 00:25:04,060
is going to start rotting,

759
00:25:04,120 --> 00:25:04,740
and when it rots,

760
00:25:04,820 --> 00:25:06,160
that's going to irritate the mucosa.

761
00:25:06,460 --> 00:25:08,940
So this rotting food in achalasia

762
00:25:08,940 --> 00:25:10,200
is going to increase the risk

763
00:25:10,200 --> 00:25:12,860
for squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus.

764
00:25:13,520 --> 00:25:15,080
Esophageal webs can also do it.

765
00:25:15,180 --> 00:25:16,380
Of course, the same idea.

766
00:25:16,720 --> 00:25:19,780
An esophageal web is a protrusion of the mucosa,

767
00:25:20,200 --> 00:25:22,820
which then results in a blocking of food,

768
00:25:22,940 --> 00:25:25,000
particularly when it is poorly chewed.

769
00:25:25,240 --> 00:25:26,880
Now, of course, food is going to pile up

770
00:25:26,880 --> 00:25:27,700
along this web,

771
00:25:27,760 --> 00:25:29,500
and when food piles up along the web,

772
00:25:29,580 --> 00:25:30,280
it's going to rot,

773
00:25:30,360 --> 00:25:31,200
and when it rots,

774
00:25:31,440 --> 00:25:33,420
that then is going to irritate the mucosa.

775
00:25:33,740 --> 00:25:35,160
So there's going to be an increased risk

776
00:25:35,160 --> 00:25:36,380
of squamous cell carcinoma.

777
00:25:36,380 --> 00:25:39,200
And the last risk factor is esophageal injury.

778
00:25:39,580 --> 00:25:41,920
Any patient that has had severe esophageal injury,

779
00:25:42,040 --> 00:25:43,440
for example, lie ingestion,

780
00:25:44,100 --> 00:25:46,520
lie is a chemical that can be used to straighten hair,

781
00:25:46,860 --> 00:25:49,200
and so this chemical could be taken,

782
00:25:49,360 --> 00:25:51,040
for example, by a child by accident,

783
00:25:51,140 --> 00:25:53,240
which would then injure the esophagus,

784
00:25:53,360 --> 00:25:55,420
increasing the risk for squamous cell carcinoma.

785
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:57,960
Now, important about esophageal carcinoma,

786
00:25:58,080 --> 00:25:58,980
whether it be squamous

787
00:25:58,980 --> 00:26:00,500
or whether it be adenocarcinoma,

788
00:26:00,840 --> 00:26:02,580
it presents very late in the course.

789
00:26:02,680 --> 00:26:04,320
The patients get progressive dysphagia.

790
00:26:04,320 --> 00:26:05,880
What does progressive dysphagia mean?

791
00:26:06,240 --> 00:26:07,360
Progressive dysphagia means

792
00:26:07,360 --> 00:26:10,380
that the dysphagia starts as dysphagia for solids,

793
00:26:10,700 --> 00:26:13,120
and eventually progresses to dysphagia

794
00:26:13,120 --> 00:26:14,720
for both solids and liquids.

795
00:26:15,120 --> 00:26:16,800
So here's a picture of the esophagus,

796
00:26:16,860 --> 00:26:17,520
and you can imagine

797
00:26:17,520 --> 00:26:19,440
that you're growing this tumor in the esophagus.

798
00:26:19,800 --> 00:26:20,300
So initially,

799
00:26:20,520 --> 00:26:22,320
the solids are going to have trouble

800
00:26:22,320 --> 00:26:23,260
passing that tumor,

801
00:26:23,580 --> 00:26:24,060
but eventually,

802
00:26:24,160 --> 00:26:25,540
the tumor is going to become larger,

803
00:26:25,640 --> 00:26:26,640
and as it expands,

804
00:26:26,880 --> 00:26:29,100
that's going to then result in dysphagia

805
00:26:29,100 --> 00:26:30,660
for both solids and liquids.

806
00:26:31,120 --> 00:26:32,680
The patients will present with weight loss,

807
00:26:32,680 --> 00:26:34,400
which is a classic finding in cancer.

808
00:26:34,680 --> 00:26:35,540
There will be pain,

809
00:26:35,640 --> 00:26:36,800
and there could be hematemesis.

810
00:26:36,960 --> 00:26:37,300
Of course,

811
00:26:37,520 --> 00:26:38,560
as the cancer grows,

812
00:26:38,620 --> 00:26:40,600
it's going to expand and stretch the mucosa,

813
00:26:40,900 --> 00:26:42,040
which would result in bleeding

814
00:26:42,040 --> 00:26:44,040
and blood in the vomit or hematemesis.

815
00:26:44,680 --> 00:26:45,020
Now,

816
00:26:45,280 --> 00:26:47,740
in the case of squamous cell carcinoma,

817
00:26:47,860 --> 00:26:49,260
we should additionally recognize

818
00:26:49,260 --> 00:26:50,660
that these patients can present

819
00:26:50,660 --> 00:26:52,560
with hoarse voice and cough,

820
00:26:52,700 --> 00:26:54,580
and the reason they can present with hoarse voice

821
00:26:54,580 --> 00:26:55,900
is because the tumor is up

822
00:26:55,900 --> 00:26:57,580
in the upper part of the esophagus,

823
00:26:57,580 --> 00:26:59,380
and that can result in invasion

824
00:26:59,380 --> 00:27:00,280
through the wall

825
00:27:00,280 --> 00:27:01,880
into the recurrent laryngeal nerve,

826
00:27:02,160 --> 00:27:03,280
and you can also get invasion

827
00:27:03,280 --> 00:27:04,700
through the wall into the trachea,

828
00:27:04,860 --> 00:27:06,320
which would then present with cough.

829
00:27:06,920 --> 00:27:09,120
When patients have esophageal carcinoma,

830
00:27:09,420 --> 00:27:10,260
the cancer will,

831
00:27:10,460 --> 00:27:11,160
as it grows

832
00:27:11,160 --> 00:27:14,200
and eventually picks up additional mutations,

833
00:27:14,380 --> 00:27:15,800
can eventually metastasize,

834
00:27:16,140 --> 00:27:17,220
and it's important to note

835
00:27:17,220 --> 00:27:18,460
there are different regions

836
00:27:18,460 --> 00:27:19,960
of lymph node spread

837
00:27:19,960 --> 00:27:22,080
based on which part of the esophagus we're in.

838
00:27:22,320 --> 00:27:23,160
So this gets back

839
00:27:23,160 --> 00:27:24,120
to a little bit of anatomy,

840
00:27:24,320 --> 00:27:25,880
and so examiners like to go after it

841
00:27:25,880 --> 00:27:27,660
because it's an anatomy tie-in.

842
00:27:27,660 --> 00:27:30,120
If the cancer arises in the upper one-third,

843
00:27:30,240 --> 00:27:31,960
it tends to spread to the cervical nodes.

844
00:27:32,280 --> 00:27:33,200
In the middle one-third,

845
00:27:33,340 --> 00:27:34,300
you'd expect it to spread

846
00:27:34,300 --> 00:27:36,740
to the mediastinal or tracheobronchial nodes,

847
00:27:36,900 --> 00:27:38,360
and if it's in the lower one-third,

848
00:27:38,480 --> 00:27:39,480
you'd expect it to spread

849
00:27:39,480 --> 00:27:41,700
to the celiac or the gastric nodes.

850
00:27:42,240 --> 00:27:44,020
And that's important to note again

851
00:27:44,020 --> 00:27:45,660
because it's an anatomy tie-in.

852
00:27:46,040 --> 00:27:48,140
So that concludes our discussion on esophagus,

853
00:27:48,200 --> 00:27:50,340
and our next section will be on the stomach.

